WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.075 --> 00:00:11.526
Rachel Okafor: Hey Luis, hey Nate. Thanks for making time. I've got Leah on with us, she's going to drive the config today.

2
00:00:11.526 --> 00:00:13.136
Rachel Okafor: How's Monday treating you?

3
00:00:13.364 --> 00:00:22.941
Luis Ortega: Busy, but good. We had a spike in claims over the weekend with that storm front, so the timing on this is decent.

4
00:00:23.313 --> 00:00:28.809
Nate Simmons: Yeah, after-hours held up fine, which is kind of why we're here. Ready when you are.

5
00:00:29.042 --> 00:00:42.194
Leah Marsh: Perfect. So the goal today is to take what's working after hours and extend it into daytime overflow. I want to walk the intents, the transfer targets, and then the Salesforce write-back at the end.

6
00:00:42.194 --> 00:00:42.946
Leah Marsh: Sound okay?

7
00:00:44.186 --> 00:00:45.203
Luis Ortega: Works for us.

8
00:00:46.048 --> 00:00:54.270
Leah Marsh: Great. Right now the agent is handling claims intake after hours, so FNOL basics, policy lookup, and then a transfer to an on-call adjuster.

9
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.695
Leah Marsh: For daytime overflow, which queues should it pick up from?

10
00:00:58.256 --> 00:01:06.558
Nate Simmons: Start with the general claims line and the policyholder services queue. Those are the two that spill over most days between eleven and two.

11
00:01:07.043 --> 00:01:12.229
Leah Marsh: Got it. And when the agent can't resolve, where should it hand off?

12
00:01:12.894 --> 00:01:19.496
Nate Simmons: Same as after hours, the claims queue in Genesys. If nobody's available it should offer a callback rather than hold.

13
00:01:20.651 --> 00:01:31.227
Leah Marsh: Okay. Let's talk intents. After hours we've got FNOL, policy status, and billing. What else do you get during the day that we should cover?

14
00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:44.076
Luis Ortega: Glass. Glass claims are a big chunk. We need the voice agent to tell a glass claim from a collision claim and send glass straight to our glass vendor's line.

15
00:01:44.559 --> 00:01:48.689
Leah Marsh: So a separate transfer target for glass, not the claims queue.

16
00:01:49.634 --> 00:02:00.020
Luis Ortega: Right. They have their own intake team. If we route glass into the main claims queue it just bounces back to us and we call the vendor anyway.

17
00:02:01.292 --> 00:02:06.941
Leah Marsh: How does the caller describe it usually? Do they say glass, or windshield, or something else?

18
00:02:07.516 --> 00:02:12.161
Luis Ortega: Windshield, mostly. Sometimes back glass. A few say chip or crack.

19
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:25.039
Leah Marsh: Okay, so I'd build a glass signal with those phrases and set the transfer target to the vendor number. I'll need the number and the hours from you, Luis.

20
00:02:25.839 --> 00:02:28.206
Luis Ortega: I'll send it over this afternoon.

21
00:02:29.527 --> 00:02:37.457
Rachel Okafor: And just so we're clear on scope, we're not touching the after-hours flow today. This is a daytime config only.

22
00:02:38.601 --> 00:02:39.401
Nate Simmons: That's fine.

23
00:02:40.366 --> 00:02:48.221
Leah Marsh: One thing I want to check before we go further. The agent is passing the caller's digital journey through PreSense on the after-hours flow.

24
00:02:48.221 --> 00:02:51.167
Leah Marsh: Do you want that on the overflow transfers too?

25
00:02:52.306 --> 00:02:59.454
Nate Simmons: Yes. Our agents lean on that a lot, especially when somebody's been on the site looking at a specific policy.

26
00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:10.067
Leah Marsh: Okay. Now, the write-back. After hours, the call outcome goes to the Case object. Is that still where you want it for overflow?

27
00:03:10.983 --> 00:03:22.241
Nate Simmons: That's the thing. The Salesforce sync is dropping the claim number into the wrong field when it's on our custom Claim object. It works fine on the standard Case object.

28
00:03:23.011 --> 00:03:25.541
Leah Marsh: Okay. When did you first notice that?

29
00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:35.741
Nate Simmons: Last week sometime. We caught it doing a spot check on the overnight batch. It's not every record, maybe one in five.

30
00:03:36.793 --> 00:03:43.660
Leah Marsh: Is it only on the voice agent calls, or are you seeing it from regular tracked calls too?

31
00:03:44.883 --> 00:03:51.548
Nate Simmons: As far as I can tell it's specific to the agent calls. The manual ones look fine.

32
00:03:51.771 --> 00:04:00.952
Leah Marsh: Can you send me a couple of example records? Call ID and the claim number, and I'll take it back to our team.

33
00:04:01.442 --> 00:04:05.431
Nate Simmons: Yeah, I'll pull three or four and send them today.

34
00:04:06.736 --> 00:04:12.785
Leah Marsh: Appreciate it. I'm not going to guess at what's happening, we'll get it looked at properly.

35
00:04:13.759 --> 00:04:21.623
Rachel Okafor: Nate, do you want to hold the overflow go-live until that's sorted, or run it and reconcile after?

36
00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:29.522
Nate Simmons: I'd rather wait until we know what's going on. Luis can still test on his side.

37
00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:37.804
Luis Ortega: Fine by me. I'll run test calls into the glass line once Leah has it set up.

38
00:04:38.422 --> 00:04:47.692
Leah Marsh: Good. And what about the AI Messaging Agent? Do you want the overflow flow to trigger a follow-up text when someone abandons before a transfer?

39
00:04:48.901 --> 00:04:53.200
Nate Simmons: Let's leave messaging out of this for now. One change at a time.

40
00:04:54.518 --> 00:05:09.311
Leah Marsh: Understood. So the build is: daytime overflow on two queues, glass intent routed to the vendor, PreSense on, callback offer when no one's available, and the Salesforce write-back stays as-is until we hear back on the claim number issue.

41
00:05:10.625 --> 00:05:11.477
Luis Ortega: That's right.

42
00:05:12.261 --> 00:05:20.962
Leah Marsh: I'll have the draft config to you by Wednesday, then we can do a test call Thursday if that works.

43
00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:23.953
Luis Ortega: Thursday afternoon is better for me.

44
00:05:25.143 --> 00:05:27.950
Leah Marsh: I'll send a hold for two o'clock.

45
00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:37.343
Rachel Okafor: I'll put the notes in the shared doc and flag the Salesforce item so it doesn't get lost. Nate, Luis, anything else before we wrap?

46
00:05:37.631 --> 00:05:39.042
Nate Simmons: Nope, that covers it.

47
00:05:39.318 --> 00:05:40.704
Luis Ortega: All good. Thanks, both.

48
00:05:41.539 --> 00:05:43.283
Rachel Okafor: Thanks everyone. Talk Thursday.
